The earlier low-down-norm candidates were wrong: neurons 11 and 3 are layer 22's two largest contributors. Good thing I measured.

The truly quiet neurons are 2525, 1409, 1878, 2593, 3581, 2919 and 2126. Their mean contribution is about 0.005 and their maximum 0.10–0.14, against a residual norm of about 10. I'll use these and avoid the spiky 3037 and 2015.

Next, the gate probe. Pass-2-only probes transferred badly, so I test whether pooling pass-2 h22 data with pass-4 x_mid data improves held-out-wording recall on pass 4. If pooling helps clearly, phrasing diversity is the lever, and a data pass with many new wordings is worth spending.

```python
# Hypothesis: pooling pass-2 (other wordings) with pass-4 improves held-out-wording recall at a code/math/mc-safe threshold.
def lowo_pooled(lbl, wcol, lam, use_p2=True):
    rec=[];fp=[];out={}
    for wd in sorted(PM.loc[PM[lbl],wcol].unique()):
        held=PM.i.isin(set(PM[(PM[lbl])&(PM[wcol]==wd)].i)).values
        tr=TA&~held
        dfs=[PM[tr]]; Xs=[X[tr]]
        if use_p2: dfs.append(PM2[TA2]); Xs.append(FX[TA2])
        beta,b=fit_on(dfs,Xs,lbl,lam); s=X@beta+b; thr=np.percentile(s[A_&NEG3],99.5)
        rec.append((s[held&A_]>thr).mean()); fp.append((s[A_&~PM[lbl].values&~NEG3]>thr).mean())
    return np.mean(rec), np.min(rec), np.mean(fp)
for lbl,wc in [('NC','wNC'),('LC','wLC')]:
    for lam in [0.1,0.3]:
        print(lbl, lam, 'pass4 only', np.round(lowo_pooled(lbl,wc,lam,False),3), '| pooled', np.round(lowo_pooled(lbl,wc,lam,True),3))
```

